\label{ch:query-based}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:query-based-intro}

Traditional compiler optimization is organized as a sequence of \emph{passes}: each pass transforms the IR in some way, and the passes are run in a fixed order. This pass-based architecture is simple and modular but has significant limitations: passes cannot easily share information, the order of passes matters and is hard to determine, and adding new optimizations requires understanding how they interact with all existing passes.

\emph{Query-based optimization} offers an alternative: instead of running passes, the compiler maintains a database of facts about the program and answers queries about those facts. Optimizations are expressed as queries that pattern-match on the database and produce transformations. This approach enables more flexible, composable, and maintainable optimization pipelines.

This chapter explores the application of database query techniques---particularly Datalog---to compiler optimization, and compares the query-based approach with traditional pass-based optimization.

For a rule language that computes program facts, start with the least
fixed point in \cref{ch:ext-343,ch:ext-344}. Then follow the compiler
example in \cref{ch:ext-351} to see where inference ends and a rewrite's
other obligations begin. A derived fact is only as sound as the rules and
the program facts that were supplied to them.

\section{Optimization as Query Answering}\label{sec:opt-as-query}

\subsection{The Query Perspective}\label{sec:query-perspective}

Every compiler optimization can be viewed as answering a question about the program:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Constant propagation}: ``What is the value of variable $x$ at program point $p$?''
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination}: ``Is the result of instruction $i$ ever used?''
  \item \textbf{Common subexpression elimination}: ``Is there another instruction that computes the same value as instruction $i$?''
  \item \textbf{Loop-invariant code motion}: ``Does the value of expression $e$ change within loop $L$?''
\end{itemize}

In the query-based view, these questions are expressed as queries over a database of program facts, and the answers drive the transformations.

\subsection{Facts and Rules}\label{sec:facts-rules}

\begin{definition}[Program Facts]\label{def:program-facts}
\emph{Program facts} are atomic statements about the program, such as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{Defines(instr, var)}: instruction \texttt{instr} defines variable \texttt{var}.
  \item \texttt{Uses(instr, var)}: instruction \texttt{instr} uses variable \texttt{var}.
  \item \texttt{CfgEdge(bb1, bb2)}: there is a control-flow edge from \texttt{bb1} to \texttt{bb2}.
  \item \texttt{Type(var, type)}: variable \texttt{var} has type \texttt{type}.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Derivation Rules]\label{def:derivation-rules}
\emph{Derivation rules} (or \emph{inference rules}) derive new facts from existing ones:
\begin{verbatim}
  ReachesDef(instr1, var, instr2) :-
    Defines(instr1, var),
    Uses(instr2, var),
    !KilledBetween(instr1, var, instr2).
\end{verbatim}
This rule says: \texttt{instr1}'s definition of \texttt{var} reaches \texttt{instr2} if \texttt{instr1} defines \texttt{var}, \texttt{instr2} uses \texttt{var}, and the definition is not killed between them.
\end{definition}

\section{Datalog for Program Analysis}\label{sec:datalog}

\subsection{Datalog Basics}\label{sec:datalog-basics}

\begin{definition}[Datalog]\label{def:datalog}
\emph{Datalog} is a declarative logic programming language that is a subset of Prolog. It consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Facts}: ground atoms (e.g., \texttt{Edge(a, b)}).
  \item \textbf{Rules}: implications of the form $\text{head} \gets \text{body}_1, \ldots, \text{body}_n$.
  \item \textbf{Queries}: goals to be evaluated against the facts and rules.
\end{itemize}
Datalog programs are guaranteed to terminate (no function symbols, only finite domains) and their semantics is given by the least fixed point of the rules.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Datalog for Dataflow Analysis}\label{sec:datalog-dataflow}

Dataflow analyses can be expressed naturally in Datalog:

\begin{example}[Reaching Definitions in Datalog]\label{ex:reaching-datalog}
\begin{verbatim}
  % Base facts
  Defines(i1, x). Uses(i2, x). CfgEdge(bb1, bb2).
  InBlock(i1, bb1). InBlock(i2, bb2).

  % Reaching definitions
  ReachesDef(i1, x, i2) :-
    Defines(i1, x), Uses(i2, x),
    InBlock(i1, bb), InBlock(i2, bb),
    DomBefore(bb, bb).  % i1 dominates i2's block

  % Transitive reachability through CFG
  ReachesDef(i1, x, i3) :-
    ReachesDef(i1, x, i2),
    CfgEdge(bb2, bb3),
    InBlock(i2, bb2), InBlock(i3, bb3),
    !Defines(i_kill, x), InBlock(i_kill, bb2).
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Datalog for Pointer Analysis}\label{sec:datalog-pointer}

Pointer analysis is naturally expressed in Datalog:

\begin{example}[Andersen's Pointer Analysis in Datalog]\label{ex:andersen-datalog}
\begin{verbatim}
  % Address-of: p = &q
  PointsTo(p, q) :- AddrOf(p, q).

  % Assignment: p = q
  PointsTo(p, t) :- Assign(p, q), PointsTo(q, t).

  % Store: *p = q
  PointsTo(t, s) :- Store(p, q), PointsTo(p, t), PointsTo(q, s).

  % Load: p = *q
  PointsTo(p, t) :- Load(p, q), PointsTo(q, r), PointsTo(r, t).
\end{verbatim}
The least fixed point of these rules gives the points-to relation.
\end{example}

\section{Rewrite Rules as Queries}\label{sec:rewrite-rules}

\subsection{Pattern Matching on IR}\label{sec:pattern-matching}

Optimizations can be expressed as rewrite rules that pattern-match on the IR:

\begin{definition}[Rewrite Rule]\label{def:rewrite-rule}
A \emph{rewrite rule} is a pair $(pattern, replacement)$ where $pattern$ is a query that matches a subgraph of the IR and $replacement$ is a transformation that replaces the matched subgraph with an optimized version.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Algebraic Simplification as Rewrite Rules]\label{ex:algebraic-rewrite}
\begin{verbatim}
  ; Rule: x + 0 => x
  Match: Add(x, Const(0)) => Replace(x)

  ; Rule: x * 1 => x
  Match: Mul(x, Const(1)) => Replace(x)

  ; Rule: x * 0 => 0
  Match: Mul(x, Const(0)) => Replace(Const(0))

  ; Rule: x - x => 0
  Match: Sub(x, x) => Replace(Const(0))
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Conditional Rewrites}\label{sec:conditional-rewrites}

Rewrites may have side conditions:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Rule: (x << n) >> n => x (if n < bitwidth and x is non-negative)
  Match: Shr(Shl(x, Const(n)), Const(n))
  Condition: n < bitwidth(x), isNonNegative(x)
  Replace: x
\end{verbatim}

\section{Pattern Matching on IR Graphs}\label{sec:graph-matching}

\subsection{Subgraph Isomorphism}\label{sec:subgraph-iso}

Pattern matching on IR graphs is a form of \emph{subgraph isomorphism}: finding a mapping from the pattern graph to a subgraph of the IR graph that preserves edges and node labels.

\begin{definition}[Subgraph Isomorphism]\label{def:subgraph-iso}
Given a pattern graph $P$ and an IR graph $G$, a \emph{match} is an injective mapping $m: V(P) \to V(G)$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For each node $v \in V(P)$, $\text{label}(v) = \text{label}(m(v))$.
  \item For each edge $(u, v) \in E(P)$, $(m(u), m(v)) \in E(G)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Subgraph isomorphism is NP-complete in general, but IR patterns are typically small and tree-like, making practical matching efficient.

\subsection{Tree Pattern Matching}\label{sec:tree-matching}

For tree-structured patterns (which cover most algebraic simplifications), matching can be done in linear time using tree pattern matching algorithms:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Top-down}: match the root, then recursively match children.
  \item \textbf{Bottom-up}: assign states to nodes using a tree automaton.
  \item \textbf{LMN (Lloyd-Lloyd-McKeeman)}: a bottom-up algorithm used in instruction selection.
\end{itemize}

\section{Soufflé and Other Datalog Engines}\label{sec:datalog-engines}

\subsection{Soufflé}\label{sec:souffle}

\begin{definition}[Soufflé]\label{def:souffle}
\emph{Soufflé} is a high-performance Datalog engine designed for program analysis. It compiles Datalog programs to optimized C++ code and supports both in-memory and out-of-core execution.
\end{definition}

Soufflé's features include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type system}: ensures that relations are used consistently.
  \item \textbf{Aggregates}: count, min, max, sum over relations.
  \item \textbf{Functors}: call external C++ functions from Datalog rules.
  \item \textbf{Parallelism}: automatic parallelization of rule evaluation.
  \item \textbf{Component system}: modular Datalog programs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Other Datalog Engines}\label{sec:other-engines}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{DOOP}: a points-to analysis framework built on Datalog.
  \item \textbf{LogicBlox}: a commercial Datalog engine with incremental evaluation.
  \item \textbf{Racket's datalog}: a Datalog implementation embedded in Racket.
  \item \textbf{Galois}: a graph analytics platform that can express Datalog-like queries.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Applying Datalog to Compiler Optimization}\label{sec:datalog-opt}

Several research projects have applied Datalog to compiler optimization:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Optix} (2017): expresses optimizations as Datalog rules over a program database.
  \item \textbf{Bril/Datalog}: uses Datalog for analysis and optimization of the Bril educational IR.
  \item \textbf{Relational compiler frameworks}: represent the entire compilation pipeline as Datalog rules.
\end{itemize}

\section{Comparison with Traditional Pass-Based Optimization}\label{sec:comparison}

\subsection{Pass-Based Architecture}\label{sec:pass-based}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Structure}: a sequence of passes, each transforming the IR.
  \item \textbf{Communication}: passes communicate through the IR (implicit) and pass-specific data structures (explicit).
  \item \textbf{Ordering}: the order of passes matters and is determined empirically.
  \item \textbf{Modularity}: each pass is independent, but interactions are hard to reason about.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Query-Based Architecture}\label{sec:query-based-arch}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Structure}: a database of facts and a set of queries/rules.
  \item \textbf{Communication}: all information flows through the database (explicit).
  \item \textbf{Ordering}: rules are evaluated to a fixed point; order does not matter.
  \item \textbf{Modularity}: rules are independent and composable; interactions are through shared facts.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Pass-based vs.\ query-based optimization}
\label{tab:pass-vs-query}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Pass-Based} & \textbf{Query-Based} \\
\hline
Composition & Sequential & Declarative \\
Iteration & Fixed pipeline & Fixed point \\
Information sharing & Implicit (IR) & Explicit (database) \\
Adding optimizations & New pass & New rule \\
Performance & Predictable & Depends on engine \\
Debugging & Step through passes & Query the database \\
Scalability & Good & Depends on engine \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Hybrid Approaches}\label{sec:hybrid}

In practice, the best systems may combine both approaches:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Use Datalog for analyses (pointer analysis, call graph construction, dataflow analysis).
  \item Use traditional passes for transformations (instruction selection, register allocation).
  \item Use equality saturation (\cref{ch:equality-saturation}) for local optimizations.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Datalog for Compilers}\label{sec:advanced-datalog}

\subsection{Interprocedural Analysis in Datalog}\label{sec:interprocedural-datalog}

Datalog excels at interprocedural analysis because it naturally handles recursive relationships across function boundaries:

\begin{example}[Interprocedural Reachability in Datalog]\label{ex:interprocedural-datalog}
\begin{verbatim}
  % Call graph edges
  Calls(f, g) :- Instr(i, _, 'call', _), InFunc(i, f), Operand(i, _, g).
  
  % Interprocedural reaching definitions
  InterReachesDef(i1, x, i2) :-
    ReachesDef(i1, x, i2).  % Intraprocedural base case
  
  % Through call edges: definition reaches through a call if it reaches the call site
  InterReachesDef(i1, x, i2) :-
    InterReachesDef(i1, x, call_instr),
    Calls(caller, callee),
    InFunc(call_instr, caller),
    InFunc(i2, callee),
    IsParam(i2, x).  % x is a parameter of the callee
  
  % Through return edges: definition reaches back from callee to caller
  InterReachesDef(i1, x, i2) :-
    InFunc(i1, callee),
    Calls(caller, callee),
    InterReachesDef(i1, x, ret_instr),
    InFunc(ret_instr, callee),
    Uses(i2, x), InFunc(i2, caller),
    Dominates(call_instr, i2).
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Demand-Driven Datalog Evaluation}\label{sec:demand-datalog}

Instead of computing all facts (bottom-up evaluation), demand-driven Datalog computes only the facts that are actually queried:

\begin{definition}[Demand-Driven Datalog]\label{def:demand-datalog}
\emph{Demand-driven Datalog} evaluates rules top-down from a query, computing only the facts needed to answer the query. This is analogous to lazy evaluation in functional programming.
\end{definition}

Benefits for compilers:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Only the analysis results actually needed by the optimizer are computed.
  \item Large programs with many functions do not require analyzing all functions.
  \item Queries like ``does definition $d$ reach use $u$?'' can be answered without computing the full reaching-definitions relation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Pattern Matching on IR}\label{sec:advanced-pattern-matching}

\subsection{Combinatorial Pattern Matching}\label{sec:combinatorial-matching}

When multiple rewrite rules can apply to the same IR node, the compiler must decide which rule to apply. This is a form of \emph{pattern matching with priorities}:

\begin{definition}[Combinatorial Pattern Matching]\label{def:combinatorial-matching}
\emph{Combinatorial pattern matching} selects the best matching rule from a set of rules that may overlap, using priorities, costs, or other criteria.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Combinatorial Pattern Matching for Rewrites}
\label{alg:combinatorial-matching}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{SelectRewrite}{$\text{node}, \text{rules}$}
  \Let{matches}{\emptyset}
  \For{each rule $r$ in rules}
    \If{\textsc{Matches}(r.pattern, node)}
      \If{\textsc{CheckConditions}(r.conditions, node)}
        \State $\text{matches} \gets \text{matches} \cup \{r\}$
      \EndIf
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \If{matches is empty}
    \State \Return \textbf{no\_rewrite}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return \textsc{SelectBest}(matches, \text{cost\_model})
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Graph Grammar Matching}\label{sec:graph-grammar}

For graph-structured IRs, pattern matching is based on \emph{graph grammars}:

\begin{definition}[Graph Grammar]\label{def:graph-grammar}
A \emph{graph grammar} is a set of rules of the form $L \to R$, where $L$ (the left-hand side) is a pattern graph and $R$ (the right-hand side) is a replacement graph. A rule applies if $L$ is isomorphic to a subgraph of the IR graph.
\end{definition}

Graph grammar matching is used in:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Instruction selection}: tree-pattern matching on expression trees (the Burg framework).
  \item \textbf{Peephole optimization}: matching small patterns in the instruction stream.
  \item \textbf{Equality saturation}: matching e-graph patterns to discover equivalent expressions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rewrite Rule Composition}\label{sec:rewrite-composition}

Multiple rewrite rules can be composed into a single, more powerful rule:

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Rule 1: (x + y) + z => x + (y + z)  [associativity]
  ; Rule 2: x + 0 => x                    [identity]
  ; Composed: (x + y) + 0 => x + y        [compose 1 then 2]
  ;
  ; This composed rule can match in a single pass,
  ; whereas applying rules 1 and 2 separately requires two passes.
\end{verbatim}

\section{Datalog for Whole-Program Optimization}\label{sec:datalog-whole-program}

\subsection{Call Graph Construction}\label{sec:datalog-callgraph}

The call graph is a fundamental whole-program structure that Datalog can compute declaratively:

\begin{verbatim}
  % Direct call graph
  DirectCall(f, g) :-
    Instr(i, _, 'call', _),
    InFunc(i, f),
    Operand(i, _, g),
    Function(g, _, _).
  
  % Class hierarchy analysis (CHA) for virtual calls
  CHACall(f, g) :-
    Instr(i, _, 'invoke_virtual', _),
    InFunc(i, f),
    Operand(i, 0, recv),
    Type(recv, class_C),
    Method(g, class_D, name, _),
    Subclass(class_D, class_C),  % class_D is a subclass of class_C
    !Overrides(class_D, name, other_g),
    other_g != g.
  
  % Transitively reachable functions
  Reachable(f) :- EntryFunction(f).
  Reachable(g) :- Reachable(f), DirectCall(f, g).
  Reachable(g) :- Reachable(f), CHACall(f, g).
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Devirtualization Queries}\label{sec:datalog-devirtualization}

Datalog can identify virtual calls that can be devirtualized (converted to direct calls):

\begin{verbatim}
  % A virtual call can be devirtualized if there is exactly one target
  Devirtualize(instr, target) :-
    Instr(instr, _, 'invoke_virtual', _),
    CHACall(func_of(instr), target),
    count(CHACall(func_of(instr), _)) = 1.
  
  % A virtual call is dead if there are no possible targets
  DeadVirtualCall(instr) :-
    Instr(instr, _, 'invoke_virtual', _),
    count(CHACall(func_of(instr), _)) = 0.
\end{verbatim}

\section{Practical Query-Based Optimization}\label{sec:practical-query}

\subsection{Building a Query-Based Optimizer}\label{sec:building-query-opt}

A practical query-based optimizer consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Fact extraction}: convert the program's IR into a set of base facts (instructions, operands, CFG edges, types).
  \item \textbf{Analysis rules}: Datalog rules that derive analysis facts (reaching definitions, live variables, points-to).
  \item \textbf{Optimization rules}: rules that identify optimization opportunities (CSE candidates, dead code, strength reduction).
  \item \textbf{Transformation engine}: applies the identified transformations to the IR.
  \item \textbf{Iteration}: re-extract facts and re-evaluate rules until a fixed point is reached.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  ; Example: complete dead code elimination in Datalog
  
  % Base facts
  Defines(i, v). Uses(i, v). Instr(i, b, op, ord).
  
  % An instruction is live if it has side effects or its result is used by a live instruction
  Live(i) :- Instr(i, _, op, _), HasSideEffect(op).
  Live(i) :- Defines(i, v), Uses(j, v), Live(j).
  
  % Dead instructions are those that are not live
  Dead(i) :- Instr(i, _, _, _), !Live(i).
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Performance Considerations}\label{sec:query-perf}

Query-based optimization faces performance challenges:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Fact extraction overhead}: converting the IR to facts takes time. Mitigation: maintain facts incrementally as the IR changes.
  \item \textbf{Rule evaluation cost}: evaluating Datalog rules can be expensive for large programs. Mitigation: use optimized engines (Soufflé) with semi-naive evaluation.
  \item \textbf{Memory usage}: storing all facts in memory can be expensive. Mitigation: use out-of-core evaluation or selective materialization.
  \item \textbf{Fixed-point convergence}: some analyses may require many iterations. Mitigation: use worklist algorithms with priority ordering.
\end{itemize}

