\section{Retracting Recursive Reachability Facts After an Edit}

Semi-naive evaluation makes additions efficient, but removing an input fact is different. In a recursive relation, derived facts can appear to support one another after their sole external justification has disappeared. A conservative first implementation recomputes the answers for affected sources rather than trying to decrement an ordinary support count.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Maintain $\mathrm{Reach}(s,t)$ for all graph nodes, including a zero-length path from each node to itself. Apply a batch of edge deletions, with no insertions and no node changes. The old relation has an index by destination, and the updated edge relation has an adjacency index by source. Recompute each affected row by a fresh graph search.

\begin{sudocode}[Rebuild rows affected by edge deletions]
\Require Old reachability $R$, deleted edges $D$, new graph $G'$
\Ensure Exact reachability in $G'$ after the batch
\State $A\gets\emptyset$
\ForAll{deleted edges $(u,v)\in D$}
  \State Add $\{s:R(s,u)\}$ to $A$
  \State \Comment{Find affected sources using the old relation}
\EndFor
\ForAll{$s\in A$}
  \Let{B}{\Call{Reachable}{G',s}}
  \State Stage replacement of row $R(s,*)$ by $B$
  \State \Comment{Derive facts from surviving edges, not old rows}
\EndFor
\State Publish the staged rows together
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Let the original edges be $A\to B$, $B\to C$, and $C\to B$. Deleting $A\to B$ selects only source $A$. Its old answer $\{A,B,C\}$ is replaced by $\{A\}$. The rows for $B$ and $C$ still contain $\{B,C\}$. The two cyclic edges cannot justify reachability from the disconnected source.

\textbf{Invariant.} Any old path that no longer exists must have used a deleted edge, so its source reached that edge's tail in the old graph and is in $A$. Recomputing may redo work for paths with alternate witnesses, but it cannot keep a circular derivation without a path. The worst case is a search per source; finer retraction algorithms trade extra indexes and bookkeeping for less recomputation. If a client needs deltas, diff each replacement against its old row before publication.

