\section{Summary}\label{sec:query-summary}

Query-based optimization offers a compelling alternative to traditional pass-based optimization. By expressing program analyses and optimizations as queries over a database of program facts, the compiler gains flexibility, composability, and clarity. Datalog provides a powerful and efficient foundation for this approach, with guaranteed termination and efficient evaluation. While query-based optimization is not yet mainstream in production compilers, it is an active area of research that may reshape how we think about compiler architecture.

\begin{historical}
The idea of using logic programming for compiler optimization dates back to the 1980s, with work on using Prolog for code generation and optimization. Datalog, developed in the late 1970s and 1980s, provided a more practical foundation. The application of Datalog to pointer analysis (Whaley and Lam, 2004) demonstrated its scalability for program analysis. Soufflé (2017) brought Datalog to practical performance levels, enabling its use in real compiler pipelines. The query-based view of optimization---where analyses are queries and transformations are driven by query results---has been explored in systems like Optix and in the equality saturation framework (\cref{ch:equality-saturation}). The convergence of database technology and compiler technology is an emerging trend that may lead to fundamentally new compiler architectures.
\end{historical}
